It is set during the Texas–Indian wars, and stars John Wayne as a middle-aged Civil War veteran who, accompanied by
his adopted nephew (Jeffrey Hunter), spends years looking for his abducted niece (Natalie Wood).

13
01:07:15,652 --> 01:07:21,652
It was shot in VistaVision on Eastmancolor negative with
processing and prints by Technicolor.

14
01:12:26,086 --> 01:12:32,086
The film was a critical and commercial success.

15
01:17:36,521 --> 01:17:42,521
Since its release, it has come to be considered a masterpiece and one
of the greatest and most influential films ever made.

16
01:22:46,956 --> 01:22:52,956
It was named the greatest American Western by the American Film Institute in 2008, and it placed 12th
on the same organization's 2007 list of the 100 greatest American movies of all time.

17
01:27:57,391 --> 01:28:03,391
Entertainment Weekly also named it the best Western.

18
01:33:07,826 --> 01:33:13,826
The British Film Institute's Sight and Sound magazine ranked it as the seventh-best film of all time based on a 2012 international survey of film
critics and in 2008, the French magazine Cahiers du Cinéma ranked The Searchers number 10 in their list of the 100 best films ever made.

19
01:38:18,260 --> 01:38:24,260
In 1989, The Searchers was deemed "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant" by the United States Library of Congress,
and selected for preservation in its National Film Registry; it was one of the first 25 films selected for the registry.

20
01:43:28,695 --> 01:43:34,695
The Searchers was the first major film to have a
purpose-filmed making-of, requested by John Ford.

21
01:48:39,130 --> 01:48:45,130
It deals with most aspects of making the film, including preparation of
the site, construction of props, and filming techniques.

22
01:53:49,565 --> 01:53:55,565
The film explores themes of brother, native american, uncle, racist, prejudice.
